::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence is arithmetic?
{
=A. 4, 9, 14, 19#Correct. Each term increases by the constant difference 5.
~B. 4, 8, 16, 32#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 2.
~C. 4, 7, 12, 19#The differences are not constant.
~D. 4, 16, 64, 256#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 4.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence is geometric?
{
~A. 3, 6, 9, 12#This has a constant difference, so it is arithmetic.
=B. 3, 6, 12, 24#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 2.
~C. 3, 7, 11, 15#This has a constant difference of 4.
~D. 3, 5, 8, 12#The differences are not constant and there is no constant ratio.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
For 7, 11, 15, 19, ..., what is the common difference?
{
~A. 3#The terms do not increase by 3 each time.
~B. 7#The first term is 7, but the common difference compares consecutive terms.
=C. 4#Correct. Each term increases by 4.
~D. 11#This is the second term, not the common difference.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
For 2, 10, 50, 250, ..., what is the common ratio?
{
~A. 2#The first term is 2, but the ratio compares consecutive terms.
~B. 8#This is the first difference, not a constant ratio.
~C. 10#The second term is not the ratio.
=D. 5#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 5.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the 6th term of 5, 8, 11, 14, ...?
{
=A. 20#Correct. This arithmetic sequence has d\=3, so a_6\=5+5(3)\=20.
~B. 17#This is the 5th term, not the 6th.
~C. 23#This adds one extra common difference.
~D. 30#This multiplies instead of adding the common difference.
}

